{
"segments": [
{
"text": "Case Four.",
"start": 0.75,
"end": 1.753,
"pause": 0.6
},
{
"text": "The Sandbar at High Tide.",
"start": 2.3529999999999998,
"end": 4.0169999999999995,
"pause": 1.4
},
{
"text": "Every morning Captain Quill, the harbourmaster, chalked the day's tide times on a blackboard outside his little office. On this particular morning, as Agnes Bell walked by with a basket of scones for him, he was writing in large letters: High water at noon.",
"start": 5.417,
"end": 21.566,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"Big tide today,\" he said. \"The sea will be right up over the sandbar by Gull Rock.\"",
"start": 22.416,
"end": 27.579,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "At low tide, a long ridge of sand appears out by Gull Rock, and people go out with buckets and forks to dig for cockles. At high tide, the sandbar disappears completely, under more than a man's height of sea.",
"start": 28.429,
"end": 42.21,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The captain's pride and joy was a brass ship's compass that sat on the windowsill of his office. At half past eleven he saw it there when he went out to help a boat tie up. When he came back at half past twelve, it was gone.",
"start": 43.06,
"end": 57.225,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "By early afternoon Constable Ollie Penrose had found three people who had been near the harbour that morning.",
"start": 58.075,
"end": 64.539,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Morwenna Day had been delivering flowers to the church on the hill. The vicar said she had been with him from eleven until one, arranging lilies, and that she had talked about nothing but lilies the whole time.",
"start": 65.389,
"end": 77.528,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Pip Carew had been sweeping out the Kettle and Gull, and Agnes herself had been there with him all morning.",
"start": 78.378,
"end": 83.946,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The third was Mr Rundle, a fisherman from along the coast, who had a sandy bucket at his feet.",
"start": 84.796,
"end": 90.535,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"I wasn't anywhere near the office,\" said Mr Rundle. \"I was out on the sandbar by Gull Rock, digging for cockles from about eleven o'clock until one. You can see my bucket.\"",
"start": 91.385,
"end": 102.457,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes thought of the blackboard, and of the captain's careful handwriting.",
"start": 103.307,
"end": 107.296,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Can you solve it? Who took the brass compass?",
"start": 108.396,
"end": 110.913,
"pause": 1.2
},
{
"text": "Think about it.",
"start": 112.113,
"end": 113.137,
"pause": 0.9
},
{
"text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
"start": 114.037,
"end": 119.264,
"pause": 4.5
},
{
"text": "The Solution.",
"start": 123.764,
"end": 124.788,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Mr Rundle took the compass.",
"start": 125.888,
"end": 127.723,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "He said he had been out on the sandbar by Gull Rock from eleven until one. But the tide board said high water at noon, and the captain had said the sea would be right up over the sandbar. At high tide the sandbar is under more than a man's height of sea. Nobody could have stood there digging cockles in the middle of that tide.",
"start": 128.57299999999998,
"end": 147.773,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Morwenna had been with the vicar the whole time, and Pip had been with Agnes. Mr Rundle's story was the only one that could not be true, and he had no other account of where he had been.",
"start": 148.623,
"end": 159.439,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The compass turned up in his boat, under a coil of rope. Mr Rundle admitted he had been admiring it for months. He gave it back, and Captain Quill, who knows a sailor's longing for a good compass when he sees one, told him where to buy a second hand one in town.",
"start": 160.289,
"end": 176.31,
"pause": 0.0
}
],
